id: quiz.arch.backpressure
kind: quiz
track: architecture
title: Producer faster than consumer
question: Your job queue grows without bound and memory climbs. What is happening and what do you do?
expects:
  - bounded queue
  - backpressure
  - shed
  - queue depth
  - scale on backlog
misuse:
  - more replicas only => moves the bottleneck to the database and multiplies load
  - hpa on cpu => the consumer is IO-bound, so CPU stays low while the queue grows
related:
  - design.backpressure
  - design.bounded-queue
  - design.load-shedding
  - k8s.hpa
follow_ups:
  - once the queue is bounded, what happens to the producer?
  - what do you scale on instead of CPU?
answer: |
  Arrival rate exceeds service rate and the buffer is absorbing the difference
  until memory runs out. Bound the queue with an explicit overflow policy: slow
  the producer (backpressure), shed with 429/503, or both. Make queue depth the
  saturation metric and autoscale on backlog/in-flight, not CPU. Then check the
  real bottleneck (database connections, locks, downstream limits).
